<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The dark-theme launcher icon, matching what iOS ships as AppIcon-Dark: the
  #191814 surface, and the slab tops warmed from #FAF9F5 to #FFE8D5 in the
  night foreground so they read as warm off-white rather than glaring against
  the dark field.

  Android has no first-class dark app icon — an adaptive icon's layers are
  fixed, and the documented answer to theming is the monochrome layer. This
  works because a launcher resolves the icon in a configuration that carries
  the UI mode, and re-resolves it when that changes. VERIFIED on One UI 8.5,
  both immediately after install and later, from a cold icon cache and a warm
  one. It is not a guaranteed behaviour, and it does not need to be: a launcher
  that ignores the qualifier shows the light icon, which is what shipping
  without this would give everyone anyway.
-->
<resources>
    <color name="ic_launcher_background">#191814</color>
</resources>
